.TH PLEDGE 1 "2026-04-27" "CECS 378" "CECS 378 Buffer Overflow Lab"
.SH NAME
pledge \- affirm authorship of your exploits; required before submission
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pledge
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pledge
prints the honor pledge text \(em which includes your handle (read
from
.IR ~/.handle ,
populated by
.B "make briefing"
on first run) and today's ISO date \(em and asks you to type the
pledge back verbatim. On exact match, it prints your honor flag
(read from
.IR /srv/honor.txt ,
mode 0400 owned by
.BR oracle (8)).
On mismatch or EOF, it exits non-zero and prints nothing.

The flag value is derived from your repository name; pasting another
student's flag will fail the autograder.

Copy the printed flag into your
.B WRITEUP.md
frontmatter as
.BR honor-flag .

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Pledge affirmed; honor flag printed.
.TP
.B 1
Pledge not affirmed (mismatch, EOF, or missing handle).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.handle
Your phreaker handle. Set on first
.B "make briefing"
run.
.TP
.I /srv/honor.txt
Source of the honor flag. Mode 0400, owned by
.BR oracle (8).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mainframe (7),
.BR oracle (8)
.SH BUGS
None known. If the pledge text is harder than the exploit, you have
done it right.
